{"seq":3,"t":0.002,"campaign_id":"li-conductor","body":{"type":"hypothesis.proposed","hypothesis":{"id":"h1","parent_id":null,"round":1,"branch":"main","statement":"Among near-hull lithium thiophosphates, a lower energy above hull predicts a lower Li migration barrier, because a well-ordered lattice gives cleaner migration channels.","rationale":"Stability and ionic conductivity are usually discussed as a trade-off. If they in fact move together across this family, the search collapses to one axis and we can screen on stability alone.","prediction":"Barrier should correlate positively with energy above hull across the seed set (Spearman rho > 0.5).","falsification_criterion":"If the most stable seed does not have the lowest barrier, or the correlation is absent or inverted, the hypothesis is refuted.","batch_plan":{"n_candidates":4,"strategy":"Score every retrieved seed on both axes; no perturbations yet.","properties":["migration_barrier","energy_above_hull"]}}}}
{"seq":4,"t":0.003,"campaign_id":"li-conductor","body":{"type":"notebook.delta","entry_id":"n-h1","author":"strategist","heading":"Round 1 \u2014 stability as a proxy for mobility","text":"The literature treats stability and conductivity as competing. "}}
{"seq":5,"t":0.003,"campaign_id":"li-conductor","body":{"type":"notebook.delta","entry_id":"n-h1","author":"strategist","heading":null,"text":"If they move together in the thiophosphates, one axis would do. "}}
